"""`aspect ci bazelrc` — write a Bazel rc that configures vanilla `bazel` calls to
use Aspect's remote services and recommended optimizations.

`aspect <task>` injects its Bazel flags itself (see `feature/workflows.axl`,
`feature/deployment.axl`, and `get_bazelrc_flags` in `lib/environment.axl`). A
bare `bazel build` started from the same job gets none of that. This command
writes the equivalent where vanilla `bazel` will find it — `~/.bazelrc` is the
first user rc Bazel loads, picked up with no `--bazelrc` plumbing:

  - On an Aspect Workflows runner, `~/.bazelrc` is written whole. The runner is
    ours, nothing else configures Bazel on it, and rewriting the file is what
    keeps it in step with the runner's environment.
  - On any other CI host, the rc goes to `~/.aspect/bazelrc` and `~/.bazelrc`
    gains one `try-import` line for it, so an rc another setup step wrote
    survives and re-running never accumulates anything.

It is the in-CLI successor to `rosetta bazelrc`: the CI integrations (the
setup-aspect GitHub Action, Buildkite plugin, CircleCI orb, GitLab component)
call this instead of `rosetta` once `rosetta` is gone from the runner image.

The generated rc gets two kinds of content:

  - Unconditional flags, applied to every `bazel` call. On a Workflows runner the
    full set — generic tuning, the remote-cache endpoint (from env), and the
    runner-specific flags (repository cache, per-runner output paths, the
    Workflows-cache compression gates). Elsewhere only the generic flags (plus
    the remote-cache endpoint when its env vars are set) — nothing tied to a
    runner's mounts.

  - Opt-in `--config` sections, one per Aspect deployment this machine knows
    (`aspect auth status`) that advertises a remote cache or BES backend:

        --config=aspect-cloud          Aspect Cloud: remote cache + BES
        --config=aspect-<name>         a configured Aspect Workflows deployment
        --config=aspect-<name>-exec    the same, plus its remote execution
        --config=aspect                the default deployment (`aspect auth use`)
        --config=aspect-exec           the default deployment's `-exec` config

    Aspect Cloud's config takes its own name; a configured deployment `<name>`
    gets `aspect-<name>`. Two deployments can still land on one config name (one
    named `cloud`, or `silo` beside `silo-exec`), and Bazel would merge such
    sections and route the build to whichever host came last, so the command
    refuses to write until one is renamed. Each wires `--remote_cache` / `--bes_backend` /
    `--bes_results_url` and authenticates through the `aspect get` credential
    helper, scoped to the deployment's hosts and pointed at the binary running
    this command — it certainly exists, whereas a bare `aspect` on PATH may not
    for a later `bazel` step, and Bazel fails outright on a helper it cannot
    find. Where BES is wired the config also has Bazel upload every
    BEP-referenced file and write the compact execution log (Bazel >= 7.1)
    under the OS temp dir, which is what the Aspect backend derives per-action
    cache and timing data from. Which actions Bazel reports as BEP events
    (`--build_event_publish_all_actions`) and how long it waits for the upload
    (`--bes_upload_mode`) are left to the user. Remote execution is
    never part of the base config: it relocates every action, so it is only ever
    reached by naming `-exec`, mirroring `--remote=exec` on `aspect <task>`.
    Aspect Cloud advertises no executor, so it gets no `-exec` config.

In import mode the generated file is rewritten whole on every run, and the user
rc only ever gains the one `try-import` line, skipped when already present.
`try-import` rather than `import` so a generated file that later disappears
degrades to "no Aspect configuration" instead of failing every `bazel` call. A
`~/.bazelrc` an earlier version of this command wrote whole on such a host
(recognizable by its header) has that output stripped — the header and every
flag line this command generates — since keeping it would leave stale flags in
force; lines it cannot account for, such as another setup step's additions,
stay. Off CI (no Workflows runner, no CI env) the user rc is left alone
entirely by default: the generated file is still written, and the import line
is only added when `--import-into` names the rc explicitly.

On Bazel >= 6.3.0 flags are emitted under `common` (applies to every command);
older Bazel has no `common` pseudo-command, so they're spelled out under `build`
(inherited by test/run/coverage/cquery/aquery/fetch/…) and `query` (the one
action-relevant command that doesn't inherit `build`). Version-gated flags are
resolved against the detected Bazel version (assumed-latest when undetectable),
matching how the workflows feature gates the same flags at invocation time.

Only file output is supported — there is no stdout mode.
"""

load("@aspect//bazel/build_metadata.axl", "version_gte")
load("./private/lib/aspect_endpoint_auth.axl", "endpoint_host")
load(
    "./private/lib/environment.axl",
    "CI",
    "RemoteCache",
    "Runner",
    "WorkflowsEnvironment",
    "bes_diagnostics_upload_flags",
    "detect_ci",
    "get_bazelrc_flags",
    "get_generic_bazelrc_flags",
    "get_workflows_environment",
    "info",
    "is_aspect_workflows_runner",
)

# Bazel gained the `common` pseudo-command (flags applied to every command) in
# 6.3.0. On older Bazel a `common` line is an unrecognized-command error, so we
# spell the flags out per command instead.
_COMMON_PSEUDO_COMMAND_MIN_VERSION = "6.3.0"

# The command sections used on Bazel < 6.3.0 (no `common`). `build` is inherited
# by the commands that matter here — test, run, coverage, cquery, aquery, fetch,
# clean, mobile-install, … — so listing it covers them all. `query` is the one
# action-relevant command that does NOT inherit `build`, so it's named
# explicitly. Every flag this file emits ungated is accepted by `query` on Bazel
# < 6.3.0 (the only range this branch runs in), so emitting them all under both
# is safe — the flags that later Bazel rejects on `query` are gated to those
# newer versions, where we use `common` instead and never reach this path.
_PRE_COMMON_COMMANDS = ["build", "query"]

# First line of the generated rc. The prefix is also how a `~/.bazelrc` written
# whole by an earlier version of this command is recognized, so it must not change.
_GENERATED_HEADER_PREFIX = "# Generated by `aspect ci bazelrc`"
GENERATED_HEADER = _GENERATED_HEADER_PREFIX + " — do not edit; rewritten on every run."

# Where the generated rc lives under the home directory, and the user rc that
# imports it.
_GENERATED_RC = ".aspect/bazelrc"
_USER_RC = ".bazelrc"

# Bazel-facing Aspect endpoints are TLS gRPC.
_SCHEME = "grpcs://"

# Bazel 6.0.0 introduced the credential-helper protocol as
# `--experimental_credential_helper`; 6.4.0 dropped the prefix.
_CREDENTIAL_HELPER_MIN_VERSION = "6.0.0"
_CREDENTIAL_HELPER_UNPREFIXED_MIN_VERSION = "6.4.0"

_DEFAULT_CONFIG = "aspect"
_CONFIG_PREFIX = "aspect-"
_EXEC_SUFFIX = "-exec"
_DEFAULT_ALIAS_SOURCE = "the default-deployment alias"

# One `--config` section of the rc. `flags` are `str | (str, constraint)` like
# everywhere else; a plain `--config=<other>` entry chains to another section and
# is written as-is rather than version-resolved. `source` names what claims the
# section, for the collision error.
RcConfig = record(
    name = field(str),
    source = field(str),
    comment = field(str, default = ""),
    flags = field(list),
)

# What `ensure_import` did to the user rc.
ImportAction = enum("created", "unchanged", "appended", "replaced")

ImportResult = record(
    content = field(str),
    action = field(ImportAction),
)

def _flag_str(flag) -> str:
    """A flag is either a plain string or, for a surviving version-gated option,
    a `(flag, version_condition)` tuple from `rc.expand` — take the flag text."""
    return flag[0] if type(flag) == "tuple" else flag

def _is_config_chain(flag) -> bool:
    return type(flag) == "string" and flag.startswith("--config=")

def startup_flags_for_rc(startup_flags: list) -> list:
    """Drop the rc-suppression startup flags, which are illegal inside an rc file.

    `get_bazelrc_flags` includes `--nohome_rc` and `--nosystem_rc` because the
    workflows feature passes these as *command-line* startup flags to
    `aspect <task>`. Here the flags land in an rc the home rc imports, and Bazel
    rejects any `--no*_rc` option that appears inside a bazelrc with a fatal
    `Can't specify --nosystem_rc in the .bazelrc file` (likewise `--nohome_rc`,
    which would also be self-defeating — Bazel would be told to ignore the very
    file importing this one). These flags only suppress *other* rc files
    (`/etc/bazel.bazelrc`, `~/.bazelrc`); that's a command-line concern and
    there's nothing the generated rc can do about it, so we drop them here.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    _RC_SUPPRESSION_FLAGS = ["--nohome_rc", "--nosystem_rc", "--noworkspace_rc"]
    return [f for f in startup_flags if f not in _RC_SUPPRESSION_FLAGS]

def command_sections(bazel_version: str | None) -> list:
    """The rc command sections the build flags go under. `common` (one section,
    applies to every command) on Bazel >= 6.3.0 or when the version is unknown
    (assume latest); the explicit `build`/`query` sections on older Bazel that
    has no `common` pseudo-command.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    if bazel_version == None or version_gte(bazel_version, _COMMON_PSEUDO_COMMAND_MIN_VERSION):
        return ["common"]
    return _PRE_COMMON_COMMANDS

def config_name(deployment) -> str:
    """The `--config` name for a deployment row: Aspect Cloud's own name for the
    built-in entry, `aspect-<name>` for a configured deployment.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    return deployment.name if deployment.builtin else _CONFIG_PREFIX + deployment.name

def credential_helper_flags(hosts: list, helper: str) -> list:
    """`--credential_helper=<host>=<helper>` for each host, scoped so the helper is
    only ever invoked for a deployment's own endpoints. Version-gated tuples: the
    unprefixed spelling from 6.4.0 (the old one still works there, with a
    deprecation warning), `--experimental_credential_helper` from 6.0.0 to 6.3.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    flags = []
    for host in hosts:
        scoped = host + "=" + helper
        flags.append(("--credential_helper=" + scoped, ">=" + _CREDENTIAL_HELPER_UNPREFIXED_MIN_VERSION))
        flags.append(("--experimental_credential_helper=" + scoped, ">=" + _CREDENTIAL_HELPER_MIN_VERSION + ", <" + _CREDENTIAL_HELPER_UNPREFIXED_MIN_VERSION))
    return flags

def bes_diagnostics_flags(exec_log_path: str = "") -> list:
    """Flags for a Bazel-uploaded BES stream that carry the build diagnostics the
    Aspect backend reads: every BEP-referenced file uploaded, plus the compact
    execution log when a path is given (see `bes_diagnostics_upload_flags`).
    Per-action data comes from the execution log, so the per-action BEP events
    behind `--build_event_publish_all_actions` are not requested; Bazel's default
    of publishing only failed actions is what every consumer needs.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    return bes_diagnostics_upload_flags(exec_log_path)

def _unique_hosts(uris: list) -> list:
    hosts = []
    for uri in uris:
        host = endpoint_host(uri) if uri else ""
        if host and host not in hosts:
            hosts.append(host)
    return hosts

def _label(deployment) -> str:
    return "Aspect Cloud" if deployment.builtin else "Aspect Workflows deployment '" + deployment.name + "'"

def _login_hint(deployment) -> str:
    """How CI authenticates this deployment's config: its API-token variable,
    or the login command for a machine with a browser."""
    login = "aspect auth login" if deployment.builtin else "aspect auth login --deployment " + deployment.name
    if deployment.api_token_env:
        return "auth via " + deployment.api_token_env + " or `" + login + "`"
    return "auth via `" + login + "`"

def _base_config(d, name: str, credential_helper: str, exec_log_path: str) -> RcConfig:
    """`--config=<name>`: the deployment's remote cache and/or BES, the credential
    helper for their hosts, and the BES diagnostics flags when BES is wired."""
    flags = []
    wired = []
    if d.cache:
        flags.append("--remote_cache=" + _SCHEME + d.cache)
        wired.append("remote cache")
    if d.bes:
        flags.append("--bes_backend=" + _SCHEME + d.bes)
        if d.results_url:
            flags.append("--bes_results_url=" + d.results_url)
        wired.append("BES")
    flags.extend(credential_helper_flags(_unique_hosts([d.cache, d.bes]), credential_helper))
    if d.bes:
        flags.extend(bes_diagnostics_flags(exec_log_path))
    return RcConfig(
        name = name,
        source = _label(d),
        comment = _label(d) + ": " + " + ".join(wired) + "; " + _login_hint(d) + ".",
        flags = flags,
    )

def _exec_config(d, name: str, credential_helper: str) -> RcConfig:
    """`--config=<name>-exec`: chains to the base config and adds the executor,
    with a credential helper for its host when the base config's don't cover it."""
    flags = ["--config=" + name, "--remote_executor=" + _SCHEME + d.exec]
    exec_host = endpoint_host(d.exec)
    if exec_host not in _unique_hosts([d.cache, d.bes]):
        flags.extend(credential_helper_flags([exec_host], credential_helper))
    return RcConfig(
        name = name + _EXEC_SUFFIX,
        source = _label(d) + " (remote execution)",
        comment = "--config=" + name + " plus its remote execution.",
        flags = flags,
    )

def deployment_configs(deployments: list, credential_helper: str, exec_log_path: str = "") -> list:
    """The `--config` sections for every deployment that advertises a remote cache
    or BES backend, in `deployments` order, followed by the `aspect` /
    `aspect-exec` aliases for the default one.

    `deployments` carries `aspect.DeploymentEndpoints` rows (or anything with the
    same `name` / `builtin` / `default` / `cache` / `bes` / `exec` /
    `results_url` / `api_token_env` fields). `credential_helper` is the path Bazel
    invokes as `<helper> get`. `exec_log_path`, when set, is where a BES-wired
    config writes the compact execution log for upload (Bazel >= 7.1).

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it.
    """
    configs = []
    aliases = []
    for d in deployments:
        if not (d.cache or d.bes):
            continue
        name = config_name(d)
        configs.append(_base_config(d, name, credential_helper, exec_log_path))
        if d.exec:
            configs.append(_exec_config(d, name, credential_helper))
        if d.default:
            aliases.append(RcConfig(
                name = _DEFAULT_CONFIG,
                source = _DEFAULT_ALIAS_SOURCE,
                comment = "The default deployment (`aspect auth use`): " + _label(d) + ".",
                flags = ["--config=" + name],
            ))
            if d.exec:
                aliases.append(RcConfig(name = _DEFAULT_CONFIG + _EXEC_SUFFIX, source = _DEFAULT_ALIAS_SOURCE, flags = ["--config=" + name + _EXEC_SUFFIX]))
    return configs + aliases

def config_collisions(configs: list) -> list:
    """One message per `--config` name that more than one `RcConfig` claims, naming
    the claimants and the fix. Bazel merges same-named sections, so a collision
    would route a build to whichever deployment's host came last; the caller
    refuses to write instead.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    claimants = {}
    for c in configs:
        claimants[c.name] = claimants.get(c.name, []) + [c.source]
    return [
        "--config=%s would be written for both %s. Rename the deployment: `aspect auth remove <name>`, then `aspect auth configure <host> --name <other>`." % (name, " and ".join(sources))
        for (name, sources) in claimants.items()
        if len(sources) > 1
    ]

def render_bazelrc(startup_flags: list, build_flags: list, command_sections: list, configs: list = []) -> str:
    """Render the generated rc: the header, a `startup` line per startup flag,
    every build flag under each section in `command_sections` (`["common"]` on
    modern Bazel, `["build", "query"]` on Bazel < 6.3.0), then each `RcConfig` as
    a commented `<section>:<name>` group. Flags may be `(flag, condition)` tuples
    (the shape `rc.expand` yields for version-gated options); only the flag text
    is written."""
    lines = [
        GENERATED_HEADER,
        "# Configures vanilla `bazel` calls to use Aspect services and recommended optimizations.",
        "",
    ]
    for flag in startup_flags:
        lines.append("startup " + _flag_str(flag))
    for flag in build_flags:
        for section in command_sections:
            lines.append(section + " " + _flag_str(flag))
    for config in configs:
        lines.append("")
        if config.comment:
            lines.append("# " + config.comment)
        for flag in config.flags:
            for section in command_sections:
                lines.append(section + ":" + config.name + " " + _flag_str(flag))
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"

def import_line(generated_rc: str) -> str:
    """The `try-import` line that pulls `generated_rc` into a user rc.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    return "try-import " + generated_rc

def generated_flag_names() -> list:
    """The names (`--flag`, no value) of every flag this command can write
    unconditionally, from the same generators the rc is rendered from, with every
    environment-dependent flag present. What `strip_generated` recognizes.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    populated = WorkflowsEnvironment(
        runner = Runner(),
        remote_cache = RemoteCache(endpoint = "x", bytestream_uri_prefix = "x"),
        ci = CI(scm_repo_name = "x"),
    )
    (startup_flags, build_flags) = get_bazelrc_flags(populated, "/x")
    return [_flag_str(f).split("=")[0] for f in startup_flags + build_flags]

def strip_generated(existing: str, flag_names: list) -> str:
    """`existing` minus what an earlier version of this command wrote into it when
    it wrote the user rc whole: the header comments it opened with and every
    `startup` / `common` / `build` / `query` line whose flag is in `flag_names`.
    Lines it cannot account for — another setup step's additions — stay, so only
    flags known to be ours are ever removed. Leading blank lines are trimmed.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    kept = []
    in_header = True
    for line in existing.split("\n"):
        if in_header and (line.startswith("#") or not line.strip()):
            continue
        in_header = False
        words = line.split(" ", 1)
        if len(words) == 2 and words[0] in ["startup"] + ["common"] + _PRE_COMMON_COMMANDS and words[1].split("=")[0] in flag_names:
            continue
        kept.append(line)
    return "\n".join(kept).lstrip("\n")

def ensure_import(existing: str | None, line: str, flag_names: list) -> ImportResult:
    """The user rc content with `line` present exactly once: unchanged when it is
    already there, appended after the existing content otherwise. A file an
    earlier version of this command wrote whole (it opens with the generated
    header) first has that output stripped — see `strip_generated`, which takes
    `flag_names` — since its flags would otherwise stay in force beside the
    import. `None`/empty `existing` is a new file.

    Public (not `_`-prefixed) so `bazelrc_test.axl` can load it."""
    if not existing:
        return ImportResult(content = line + "\n", action = ImportAction("created"))
    if line in existing.split("\n"):
        return ImportResult(content = existing, action = ImportAction("unchanged"))
    action = ImportAction("appended")
    if existing.startswith(_GENERATED_HEADER_PREFIX):
        existing = strip_generated(existing, flag_names)
        action = ImportAction("replaced")
    content = (existing.rstrip("\n") + "\n" if existing.strip() else "") + line + "\n"
    return ImportResult(content = content, action = action)

_IMPORT_VERBS = {
    ImportAction("created"): "created %s with it",
    ImportAction("unchanged"): "%s already imports it",
    ImportAction("appended"): "added the import to %s",
    ImportAction("replaced"): "replaced the lines an earlier version generated in %s with the import",
}

def _home_path(ctx: TaskContext, relative: str) -> str:
    """`~/<relative>`; ends the task when the home directory can't be resolved."""
    home = ctx.std.env.home_dir()
    if home == None:
        ctx.std.process.exit(1, "Could not resolve your home directory to default the rc paths. Pass --output=<path> (and --import-into=<rc>) explicitly.")
    return home.rstrip("/") + "/" + relative

def _write(ctx: TaskContext, path: str, content: str) -> None:
    """Write `content` to `path`, creating parent directories."""
    parent = path.rstrip("/").rsplit("/", 1)
    if len(parent) == 2 and parent[0] and not ctx.std.fs.exists(parent[0]):
        ctx.std.fs.create_dir_all(parent[0])
    ctx.std.fs.write(path, content)

def _resolve(ctx: TaskContext, flags: list, bazel_version: str | None) -> list:
    """Resolve the version-gated `(flag, constraint)` tuples in `flags` against the
    detected Bazel version via the bazelrc runtime: a blank RunCommand whose
    `common` bucket holds every flag, expanded assumed-latest when the version is
    unknown. Config chain entries are kept verbatim ahead of the rest — the blank
    RunCommand has no section for them to expand into."""
    chain = [f for f in flags if _is_config_chain(f)]
    rest = [f for f in flags if not _is_config_chain(f)]
    rc = ctx.bazel.new_rc(flags = rest, version = bazel_version)
    return chain + rc.expand(command = "common")

def _exec_log_path(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """A job-scoped location for the compact execution log the BES-wired configs
    write, under the OS temp dir so nothing lands in the checkout. Namespaced by
    this run's task id, since concurrent jobs on one machine share the temp dir."""
    dir = ctx.std.env.temp_dir().rstrip("/") + "/aspect-bazelrc-" + (ctx.task.id or "exec")
    ctx.std.fs.create_dir_all(dir)
    return dir + "/exec.log.zstd"

def _resolved_configs(ctx: TaskContext, bazel_version: str | None) -> list:
    """`deployment_configs` for this machine's deployments, version-resolved.
    Reads `deployments()`, not `list()`: nothing here needs login status, and a CI
    runner may have no credential store to read."""
    deployments = ctx.aspect.auth.deployments()
    exec_log_path = _exec_log_path(ctx) if any([d.bes for d in deployments]) else ""
    configs = deployment_configs(deployments, ctx.std.env.current_exe(), exec_log_path)
    collisions = config_collisions(configs)
    if collisions:
        ctx.std.process.exit(1, "\n".join(collisions))
    return [
        RcConfig(name = c.name, source = c.source, comment = c.comment, flags = _resolve(ctx, c.flags, bazel_version))
        for c in configs
    ]

def _bazelrc_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    environment = get_workflows_environment(ctx.std)
    on_runner = is_aspect_workflows_runner(ctx.std.env)

    if on_runner:
        # Full set: generic CI flags + the runner-specific cache/output flags.
        # Gate on the runner metadata being present.
        if environment.runner == None:
            return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "Running on an Aspect Workflows runner but the runner metadata env vars are missing, so the cache flags cannot be generated. This usually means an older runner image; upgrade the runner.")
        (startup_flags, build_flags) = get_bazelrc_flags(
            environment = environment,
            aspect_root_dir = ctx.std.env.aspect_root_dir(),
        )
        startup_flags = startup_flags_for_rc(startup_flags)
    else:
        # Only the generic flags that don't depend on the runner's mounts or the
        # Workflows cache. The remote-cache endpoint flags are still picked up
        # from env vars when set. No runner-specific startup flags.
        build_flags = get_generic_bazelrc_flags(environment)
        startup_flags = []

    bazel_version = ctx.bazel.version()
    build_flags = _resolve(ctx, build_flags, bazel_version)
    configs = _resolved_configs(ctx, bazel_version)

    # A runner's user rc is written whole; elsewhere the rc is a separate file
    # the user rc imports.
    output = ctx.args.output or _home_path(ctx, _USER_RC if on_runner else _GENERATED_RC)
    _write(ctx, output, render_bazelrc(startup_flags, build_flags, command_sections(bazel_version), configs))

    summary = "Wrote Aspect bazelrc to %s (%d build flags, %d startup flags)" % (output, len(build_flags), len(startup_flags))
    if configs:
        summary += "; --config sections: " + ", ".join([c.name for c in configs])
    else:
        summary += "; no --config sections, since no deployment advertises endpoints here (see `aspect auth status`; `aspect auth login` records Aspect Cloud's)"

    # Off CI the user rc is a developer's own: only touch it when named explicitly.
    on_other_ci = not on_runner and detect_ci(ctx.std.env) != None
    user_rc = ctx.args.import_into or (_home_path(ctx, _USER_RC) if on_other_ci else None)
    if user_rc == None or user_rc == output:
        hint = "" if on_runner or user_rc == output else ". Not on CI, so no user rc was changed; add `%s` to yours to use it" % import_line(output)
        info(ctx.std, summary + hint + ".")
        return 0

    existing = ctx.std.fs.read_to_string(user_rc) if ctx.std.fs.exists(user_rc) else None
    result = ensure_import(existing, import_line(output), generated_flag_names())
    if result.action != ImportAction("unchanged"):
        _write(ctx, user_rc, result.content)
    info(ctx.std, summary + "; " + _IMPORT_VERBS[result.action] % user_rc + ".")
    return 0

bazelrc = task(
    group = ["ci"],
    summary = "Write a Bazel rc that points vanilla `bazel` calls at Aspect's remote services and recommended optimizations.",
    description = "Write a Bazel rc so vanilla `bazel` calls pick up the same configuration that `aspect <task>` injects. On an Aspect Workflows runner `~/.bazelrc` is written whole; on other CI the rc goes to `~/.aspect/bazelrc` and `~/.bazelrc` gains a single `try-import` line for it, added once. Unconditionally: the recommended CI/profiling flags, plus on a Workflows runner its remote cache, repository cache, and per-runner output paths. Opt-in `--config` sections for every Aspect deployment this machine knows (`aspect auth status`): `--config=aspect-cloud` for Aspect Cloud's remote cache + BES, `--config=aspect-<name>` for a configured deployment, `--config=aspect-<name>-exec` to add its remote execution, and `--config=aspect` for the default deployment — authenticated through the `aspect get` credential helper. Off CI the rc is still written to `~/.aspect/bazelrc` but `~/.bazelrc` is left alone unless --import-into names it.",
    implementation = _bazelrc_impl,
    args = {
        "output": args.string(
            default = "",
            description = "Rc file to write, rewritten on every run. Defaults to `~/.bazelrc` on an Aspect Workflows runner and to `~/.aspect/bazelrc` elsewhere. Parent directories are created if needed. Only file output is supported — there is no stdout mode.",
        ),
        "import_into": args.string(
            long = "import-into",
            default = "",
            description = "User rc that receives a `try-import <output>` line, added only when not already present. Defaults to `~/.bazelrc` on CI other than an Aspect Workflows runner (where the output is `~/.bazelrc` itself), and to nothing off CI, so a developer's own rc is never modified without asking.",
        ),
    },
)
